uct.\n\nWe need to pick for each line a point in column 2 (maybe some lines will not have a point in column 2). But we need to cover all points in column 2: (2,1),(2,2),(2,3). So we need to assign each of these three points to distinct lines (or possibly same line could cover multiple points in column 2? No, a line can intersect column 2 at most once, so each line can cover at most one of these points). So we need at least 3 lines to cover column 2 points. Since we have 4 lines, we can assign each of the three points to three distinct lines, leaving one line not covering any column 2 point.\n\nSimilarly, column 3 points (3,1),(3,2) need to be covered. They can be assigned to any lines, possibly the same lines that already cover column 2 points? No, a line can intersect column 3 at most once, but it can intersect column 2 also once. So a line could cover a point in column 2 and a point in column 3 (different columns). That's allowed. So we can assign (3,1) to a line that also covers a column 2 point, etc.\n\nSimilarly, column 4 point (4,1) can be assigned to a line that also covers column 2 or column 3 point.\n\nThus we need to find 4 lines each passing through a distinct column 1 point and possibly also through a point in column 2,3,4.\n\nWe need to ensure each line is sunny (slope not 0, ∞, -1). Let's try to assign:\n\n- L1: passes through (1,1) and (2,2). Slope = (2-1)/(2-1) = 1, sunny. This line also passes through (3,3) not in T_4, (4,4) not in T_4. So L1 covers (1,1) and (2,2).\n\n- L2: passes through (1,2) and (2,3). Slope = (3-2)/(2-1) = 1, sunny. L2 covers (1,2) and (2,3).\n\n- L3: passes through (1,3) and (3,2). Slope = (2-3)/(3-1) = -1/2 = -0.5, sunny. L3 covers (1,3) and (3,2).\n\n- L4: passes through (1,4) and (4,1). Slope = (1-4)/(4-1) = -3/3 = -1, which is not sunny. So we need a different point for L4. Let's try (1,4) and (3,1). Slope = (1-4)/(3-1) = -3/2 = -1.5, sunny. L4 covers (1,4) and (3,1). Then we still need to cover (2,1) and (4,1). (2,1) is n